A||||15||sqoach||In the process of a tune-up with newly acquired V6 4 spd||||7||
Z||000000||sqoach||07-05-2001||08:14 PM||||Need some advice though. It seems to be lacking power. I figured there would be a noticeable difference from my 4, but I'm not seeing it. I guess I can list what I have fixed/replaced or what has been replaced recently that I know of<BR>-new plugs and wires<BR>-new air filter<BR>-new fuel filter<BR>-oil change<BR>-getting a new tach filter<BR>That's all I know of. It seems to run good, but the idle is a little un-steady. I had my exhaust looked at to get rid of some rattles, and was told the converter needs replaced, which I figured it would cuz I checked it. I'm also getting the timing checked. What else should I look for or do? I've never worked with the V6, so good explainations help  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumsig.JPG">      <BR>84 SE (FOR SALE)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)(take it off my hands for $50)<BR>86 SC (FOR SALE sometime in this summer)<BR>86 SE V6 (new project)||||reg||7
Z||000001||87GTZ34||07-05-2001||08:31 PM||||Well it looks like you're on the right track.  How was the cap & rotor?  You should have the timing checked/set as you mentioned and that will probably be the most noticable change.  Also, run a good injector cleaner/lube through a tank of gas.  Follow the directions as more is not better with injector cleaner... damage to the injector is possible with some cleaners that are not  properly diluted.  How many miles on the car?  Also, was the cat clogged or burnt out?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||07-05-2001||08:41 PM||||The cat being plugged would account for the loss of power. If so, it's hard to see any tuning results until it's replaced or removed.<BR>I,d check the oil level in the tranny on a newly acquired car. Few People check them on a regular basis since the 86 has no obvious dip stick, it being the speedo sending unit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||07-05-2001||09:06 PM||||It has 81,+++ miles on it.<BR>Haven't check the cap and rotot yet. Something else to add to my list. The converter sounds like something's in it when I hit it. I'm thinking about just replacing it with a straight pipe to keep the costs down.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I'll check the tranny fluid tomorrow probably. A member that I chat with had mentioned that to me. Thanks guys.<P>Any other info appreciated  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||V8Fiero||07-05-2001||09:34 PM||||Funny you brought this up sqoach. I was just thinking about this today. My Gt is the 3rd V6 that I have owned. My first two seemed pretty quick (autos) but not what I thought they should be compared to my first 2m4 (auto). My first stick shift fiero (85 2m4) was really peppy and felt like it could keep up with my other V6's.<P>Following me so far???<P>Good!<P>Now this GT.....Man. It doesnt compare to the other V6's. Faster than snot and really, really pulls (107k auto)......<P>Wondering if this is just because of the gearing for the GT. There is such a big difference, there has to be more to it than that.....I know nothing of the engine that is in the GT. The original one seized up and I got the car w/the replacement from 007Doug. Just my pondering thoughts....thanks for reading....<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR>86 SE Gold<BR>86 GT Red <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RedRavensig.JPG"> <BR>"Trying to change from V8Fiero to RedRavenGT"||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||07-05-2001||10:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by V8Fiero:<BR><B>Funny you brought this up sqoach. I was just thinking about this today. My Gt is the 3rd V6 that I have owned. My first two seemed pretty quick (autos) but not what I thought they should be compared to my first 2m4 (auto). My first stick shift fiero (85 2m4) was really peppy and felt like it could keep up with my other V6's.<P>Following me so far???<P>Good!<P>Now this GT.....Man. It doesnt compare to the other V6's. Faster than snot and really, really pulls (107k auto)......<P>Wondering if this is just because of the gearing for the GT. There is such a big difference, there has to be more to it than that.....I know nothing of the engine that is in the GT. The original one seized up and I got the car w/the replacement from 007Doug. Just my pondering thoughts....thanks for reading....<P>Scott<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's sad. I was thinking I'd feel quite a bit more power, but nothing. I actually think my 4 can beat it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>The exhaust sounds a lot meaner, though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>But I've had my green SC for a while now, and know what I've changed. I just got this one, so maybe when I get some stuff fixed I'll feel the power. Hope so anyway  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||07-06-2001||11:47 AM||||You're going from about 90HP to 140HP, so yeah, you should see a big difference.  Even if you're comparing it to a 2m4 with the 4.10 4speed, the V6 should be much faster.  <P>If not, something's amiss.  When my cat plugged up, it felt like it was running fine, just no power.  It got steadily worse until it became virtually undriveable - then I noticed the glowing exhaust manifolds.  Give her a good tune up - check the exhaust for kinks and the cat.  Maybe run a compression check to make sure the engine's in good shape.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||btoth||07-06-2001||06:22 PM||||Did you heck the coil?  They tend to die faster than other cars I've found.  Might as well put in an MSD.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (Might help your idle if it's not the timing.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||sqoach||07-07-2001||12:16 PM||||Check the dist. cap and sanded a bit of corrosion off the connections. No difference that I could tell from just starting the car and letting it run for a minute. My dad said the coil looked ok. I don't know how you just look at it and make a decision, but he did  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>I'm getting the timing checked next week, and also getting a "test pipe" in place of the converter for temporary use  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Still need to check the tranny fluid. Would it be a good idea to just drain it and add new stuff? Is it expensive?||||reg||1
Z||000009||AkursedX||07-07-2001||12:37 PM||||1st off, there is no way to tell if a coil is good or not just by looking at it.  My accel coil still looks brand new, but it's definietly dead.  I'd say you buy a new coil anyways, just to have an extra one around.  If you want performance, go with the MSD coil, or one of the bigger accel coils that you can mount to the firewall.  <P>Tranny fluid is not expensive, and you should probably change the filter in it if you drain it.  It's not that expensive ~15-20 bucks I think.  <P>Another thing you could do is do a compression check.  If you do have a plugged cat, you might be risking getting burnt valves, which would definitly result in a loss of power.  A plugged cat would also contribute to a rough idle.  The straightpipe is probably a good way to go.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||sqoach||07-07-2001||04:11 PM||||Ok, if I decided to buy another coil, what's the pros and cons of going with the MSD. I figure it's more expensive. Is it worth getting? And how much are they, usually?<P>And is there a specific kind of tranny fluid I need to go with? Brand?||||reg||1
Z||000011||AkursedX||07-07-2001||04:22 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>Ok, if I decided to buy another coil, what's the pros and cons of going with the MSD. I figure it's more expensive. Is it worth getting? And how much are they, usually?<P>And is there a specific kind of tranny fluid I need to go with? Brand?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The pro's of going with the MSD coil is more voltage, so your spark plugs get more spark, (but this also depends on the condition of your wires)  You would mostly notice better performance at higher rpm's (stock coils don't fare too well at higher rpm's)  <P>The cons.  Well of course it is more expensive.  I think they are around 60 bucks.  Which is about twice the cost of a stock coil.  Also, they are still in the stock location, and there is alot of heat there.  But I have not heard of an MSD coil failing yet.  That's why you might want to consider moving the coil to the trunk-wall.  This will allow you to buy the bigger Accel coils.  I have heard good reviews about those.  <P>EDIT:  I just realized you had a standard transmission, not an automatic.  Sorry about that.  And I really don't know what you should use for that.<p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 07-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||sqoach||07-13-2001||06:46 PM||||Alright! Got the converter out. It looked half burnt out, but still had a bunch of crud in it restricting air flow. What a difference it made! I am definately feeling the power in 1st and 2nd gears. It's about the same on the highway, but is better than it was. Sounds a little meaner, too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Still need to check the tranny fluid. I can't seem to figure it out. Is it that plug that goes into the tranny close to the trunk-side? I'm just gonna drain the old stuff and put in some new. I know where the drain plug is...I think. And the tranny filter...? Never heard of that. Where's it located?||||reg||10
Z||000013||StuGood||07-13-2001||06:54 PM||||I put oil in my 4-speed at the electric speedometer sender+dipstick.  It comes out when you loosen the "barn door latch" tab thingy with a 10mm socket and swing the tab aside.<P>There's no transmission filter in a stock Fiero 4-speed, unless I've being just been overlooking it, for 15 years!  <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||07-13-2001||07:24 PM||||one of the bolts on the top rear/side of case is where you put the oil in a 4 spd. at least my 86. I just pull it out and fill it with a pump and small hose until it starts to run back out. Mine dont have any kind of dipstick.<P>about the power issue, Ive had 2 identical cars before (not Fieros) same engine, same rough miles, same trans, same rear gear, even same interior and color. One ran like a wild horse, other like a sick slug. Both were tuned the same, both started and ran perfect. Nothing i would do would make any difference. One would smoke the tires down to the rims, other was lucky to make a chirp.<BR>I think sometimes, its just luck of the draw.<BR>They both were 69 Lincoln Mark III's with 425 hp 460 ci 4 bbl<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||lowCG||07-14-2001||11:00 AM||||Give it a couple days for the negine computer to figure out the change in flow,it'll run even better,or should I'm guessing.||||reg||1
